1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,960
So good to see you.

2
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:01,679
Great to see you too.

3
00:00:01,679 --> 00:00:02,480
Last time I saw you I was in San Francisco.

4
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:02,980
Yes.

5
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:04,120
I know you are all over the place.

6
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:06,679
Yes.

7
00:00:06,679 --> 00:00:09,279
I'm curious if this year feels different than the last time

8
00:00:09,279 --> 00:00:10,839
you were in Davos.

9
00:00:10,839 --> 00:00:12,740
Gemini 3 is out.

10
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:16,640
We've heard that OpenAI called a code red internally.

11
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,359
Do you feel like Google got its mojo back?

12
00:00:20,359 --> 00:00:22,120
Well, I'm not sure if that's for me to say,

13
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:24,640
but I feel like we had a very good year.

14
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:27,359
So it's been hard work, really hard work,

15
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:32,920
getting our technology and the models back to state of the art.

16
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:35,640
I think we did that with Gemini 3 especially,

17
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:39,560
and Nanobahn, our in-imaging software.

18
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:41,939
And then I think we've also adapted, really,

19
00:00:41,939 --> 00:00:44,920
to this new world of shipping very fast,

20
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:47,200
bringing a startup energy to what we do.

21
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,439
Do you think people underestimated Google,

22
00:00:49,439 --> 00:00:50,399
or got something wrong?

23
00:00:50,399 --> 00:00:51,120
Yes, maybe.

24
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:52,079
I'm not sure.

25
00:00:52,079 --> 00:00:54,399
I think we always had the ingredients

26
00:00:54,399 --> 00:00:57,200
to be at the forefront of this.

27
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,000
Obviously, we've got the long history in it.

28
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,720
I think over the last decade, Google and DeepMind between us,

29
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:05,600
we've invented most of the breakthroughs

30
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:07,960
that the modern AI industry relies on now.

31
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,280
I see Transformers most famously, but AlphaGo,

32
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:12,879
deep reinforcement learning, these things.

33
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:15,560
And we have these incredible product surfaces, billion

34
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:19,120
user services that are natural fits for AI, actually,

35
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:23,840
from search to email to Chrome.

36
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,280
But it's just getting all of that together.

37
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:28,239
And organizing the right way.

38
00:01:28,239 --> 00:01:30,560
And I think we've done that in the last couple of years.

39
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:31,799
And there's still a lot more work to do,

40
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:33,560
but I think we're starting to see the fruits of that.

41
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:35,320
If you think you have an advantage,

42
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:37,560
how big do you think your advantage is?

43
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,799
How long does it last?

44
00:01:39,799 --> 00:01:42,879
Well, I think everything starts with research, in my opinion.

45
00:01:42,879 --> 00:01:48,000
And the models, especially, being state of the art

46
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
on all the different benchmarks.

47
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,560
And that's what we focused on first

48
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:54,000
when we put Google and DeepMind together.

49
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,280
And I think with the Gemini series,

50
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,159
we're very happy with how that's going.

51
00:01:58,159 --> 00:01:59,680
There's a lot more work to do there.

52
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:02,680
But I think we're the only organization that

53
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:06,640
has the full stack, from the TPUs and the hardware,

54
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,080
the data centers, the cloud business, the Frontier Lab,

55
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:12,039
and all of these amazing products that

56
00:02:12,039 --> 00:02:14,280
can kind of natural fits for AI.

57
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,680
So really, structurally, from first principles,

58
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:18,840
we should be doing very well.

59
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,120
And I think there's actually a lot more headroom

60
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:22,639
to come from here.

61
00:02:22,639 --> 00:02:25,559
I wonder what a day in the life of a Frontier model leading

62
00:02:25,559 --> 00:02:27,439
AI CEO is like.

63
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:29,279
I've read that you do most of your thinking

64
00:02:29,279 --> 00:02:30,519
between 1 and 4 AM.

65
00:02:30,519 --> 00:02:32,279
Yes, that's right.

66
00:02:32,279 --> 00:02:35,559
Is it ever not a code red inside?

67
00:02:35,559 --> 00:02:38,399
Do you ever feel comfortable?

68
00:02:38,399 --> 00:02:40,919
No, you never feel comfortable.

69
00:02:40,919 --> 00:02:43,759
Code reds are for very special circumstances.

70
00:02:43,759 --> 00:02:48,560
But it's always, for the last, I would say, three, four years,

71
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:51,719
it's been unbelievably intense.

72
00:02:51,759 --> 00:02:57,960
And 100 hour weeks, 50 weeks a year, and that's the norm.

73
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:00,360
And I think that's what you have to do

74
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:02,759
at the forefront of this unbelievably fast moving

75
00:03:02,759 --> 00:03:03,360
technology.

76
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:06,000
It's ferociously competitive out there.

77
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,759
Maybe the most intense competition

78
00:03:07,759 --> 00:03:09,479
has ever been in technology.

79
00:03:09,479 --> 00:03:15,639
And the stakes are incredibly high, AGI and all of that

80
00:03:15,639 --> 00:03:16,520
can mean.

81
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,639
So commercially, scientifically, and then

82
00:03:19,639 --> 00:03:22,399
if you add all the excitement of what we're doing

83
00:03:22,399 --> 00:03:25,000
and using it as my passion, as you know,

84
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,479
is exploring scientific problems with AI,

85
00:03:29,479 --> 00:03:31,599
accelerating scientific discovery itself.

86
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:33,199
This is what I've always dreamed about,

87
00:03:33,199 --> 00:03:35,719
and I've worked my whole life on AI towards this moment.

88
00:03:35,719 --> 00:03:38,159
So it's sort of hard to sleep because there's

89
00:03:38,159 --> 00:03:40,959
so much work to do, but also there's

90
00:03:40,959 --> 00:03:44,599
so much exciting things to look into and to push forward.

91
00:03:44,599 --> 00:03:48,560
I know you're very focused on AI driving scientific progress,

92
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:50,360
discovering new materials even.

93
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,080
We've seen now Gemini being integrated

94
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:53,960
into humanoid robots.

95
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:59,000
Is the alpha fold moment for the physical world in AI here?

96
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:00,840
What is that, and what does that look like?

97
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:03,280
Yeah, I spent a lot of the last year actually looking

98
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:04,840
very carefully into robotics.

99
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:09,240
I do think we're on the cusp of a kind of breakthrough moment

100
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:11,199
in physical intelligence.

101
00:04:11,199 --> 00:04:14,480
I still think we're about 18 months, two years away from

102
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:16,079
doing, we need to do more research.

103
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:19,759
But I think the foundation models like Gemini

104
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:20,680
show the way forward.

105
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:23,199
I mean, from the beginning, we made Gemini multimodal

106
00:04:23,199 --> 00:04:25,000
so it could understand the physical world

107
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:26,359
for multiple reasons.

108
00:04:26,359 --> 00:04:29,279
One was so we could build a universal assistant maybe

109
00:04:29,279 --> 00:04:31,519
that exists on your glasses or your phone that understands

110
00:04:31,519 --> 00:04:32,479
the world around you.

111
00:04:32,479 --> 00:04:35,719
But of course, a second use of that would be for robotics.

112
00:04:35,719 --> 00:04:39,479
So what does that moment for the physical world look like?

113
00:04:39,479 --> 00:04:45,719
I think it's having robots reliably do useful tasks out

114
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:46,560
in the world.

115
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:48,840
And I think there's a few things holding that back still.

116
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:51,880
Part of it is the algorithms are still not quite there.

117
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:54,280
They need a little bit more robustness.

118
00:04:54,280 --> 00:04:57,440
They have to work with less data than you

119
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:59,480
get for the LLMs or the models that

120
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:00,920
work on just digital realm.

121
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:02,560
You can sort of create synthetic data.

122
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:04,560
It's a lot harder to make that kind of data.

123
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:06,600
And there's still sort of some problems in the hardware

124
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:08,400
that are not solved, specifically things

125
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:09,640
like the arm and the hand.

126
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:11,920
Actually, when you look into robotics very carefully,

127
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:14,160
you get a newfound appreciation, at least I

128
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:17,560
for the human hand and how exquisite evolution

129
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:18,280
has designed that.

130
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:19,240
It's incredible.

131
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:22,880
And it's very hard to match the reliability, the strength,

132
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,280
and the dexterity that the human hand has.

133
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:27,920
So there's still quite a lot, in my opinion,

134
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:29,480
pieces that are put together.

135
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:30,760
But there's very exciting things.

136
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:33,040
I mean, we just announced a new deep collaboration

137
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:34,320
with Boston Dynamics.

138
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:36,480
They've got some very exciting robots in Hyundai

139
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:39,680
and actually applying it to automotive manufacturing.

140
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:41,120
And we'll see over the next year how

141
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:43,120
that goes in sort of prototype form.

142
00:05:43,199 --> 00:05:44,639
And then maybe in a year or two, we'll

143
00:05:44,639 --> 00:05:46,439
have some really impressive demonstrations

144
00:05:46,439 --> 00:05:47,759
that we can scale up.

145
00:05:47,759 --> 00:05:53,160
A year ago, DeepSeek seemed cataclysmic for the West.

146
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:56,079
Now, a year later, it's quite China.

147
00:05:56,079 --> 00:05:57,720
It seems to have been quieter.

148
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:00,959
Has your opinion on competition from China changed?

149
00:06:00,959 --> 00:06:01,759
Not really.

150
00:06:01,759 --> 00:06:04,160
I mean, I didn't think it was cataclysmic in the first place.

151
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:05,639
I think it was a massive overreaction.

152
00:06:05,639 --> 00:06:07,240
In the West, it was impressive.

153
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:11,959
And I think it shows that the Chinese are very capable,

154
00:06:11,959 --> 00:06:15,560
that the leading companies, I think companies like ByteDance,

155
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:18,799
actually, I would say, are the most capable.

156
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:22,279
And they're maybe only six months behind, not one or two

157
00:06:22,279 --> 00:06:23,839
years behind the frontier.

158
00:06:23,839 --> 00:06:25,719
So I think that's what DeepSeek showed.

159
00:06:25,719 --> 00:06:27,479
Some of the claims were over-exaggerated

160
00:06:27,479 --> 00:06:30,639
about the amount of compute they used and being so minimal

161
00:06:30,639 --> 00:06:33,479
and so on because they relied on some Western models

162
00:06:33,479 --> 00:06:36,240
and also fine-tuning on the outputs of some

163
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:37,879
of the leading Western models.

164
00:06:37,879 --> 00:06:39,959
So it wasn't sort of de novo.

165
00:06:40,599 --> 00:06:43,799
The other thing, I think, so far is not yet to be seen is,

166
00:06:43,799 --> 00:06:46,399
can China actually, the Chinese companies,

167
00:06:46,399 --> 00:06:48,719
innovate beyond the frontier themselves?

168
00:06:48,719 --> 00:06:50,519
So they're gaining.

169
00:06:50,519 --> 00:06:53,079
They're very good at kind of catching up

170
00:06:53,079 --> 00:06:56,039
to where the frontier is and increasingly capable at that.

171
00:06:56,039 --> 00:06:57,639
But I think they've yet to show they

172
00:06:57,639 --> 00:06:59,439
could innovate beyond the frontier.

173
00:06:59,439 --> 00:07:01,919
You helped define AGI.

174
00:07:01,919 --> 00:07:04,039
You have said, we have a 50% chance

175
00:07:04,039 --> 00:07:05,919
of getting there by 2030.

176
00:07:05,919 --> 00:07:07,799
Is that still your timeline?

177
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:08,679
It is.

178
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:12,840
Is AGI still a useful target for you?

179
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:13,519
I think so.

180
00:07:13,519 --> 00:07:14,720
I think it's a very useful.

181
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:16,560
That is still my timeline.

182
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:18,519
And it's a little bit longer than some others

183
00:07:18,519 --> 00:07:19,079
that you're here.

184
00:07:19,079 --> 00:07:20,280
It is longer than some others.

185
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,600
But my bar is quite high.

186
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:24,519
It's the ability to, a system that

187
00:07:24,519 --> 00:07:27,280
exhibits all the cognitive capabilities humans have.

188
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:30,519
And I think we're still clearly quite far from that.

189
00:07:30,519 --> 00:07:34,000
That means in things like scientific creativity,

190
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,759
not just solving a conjecture or solving a problem in science,

191
00:07:36,759 --> 00:07:39,639
but actually coming up with the hypothesis or the problem

192
00:07:39,639 --> 00:07:40,759
in the first place.

193
00:07:40,759 --> 00:07:43,759
And as any scientist knows, finding the right question

194
00:07:43,759 --> 00:07:46,920
is actually often way harder than finding the answer.

195
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:50,800
So it's not clear that these systems have that capability.

196
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:52,519
In fact, they definitely don't right now.

197
00:07:52,519 --> 00:07:53,719
I think they will eventually.

198
00:07:53,719 --> 00:07:55,599
But it's not clear what's needed still.

199
00:07:55,599 --> 00:07:57,319
And there's things like continual learning,

200
00:07:57,319 --> 00:07:59,959
online learning, going beyond what they've

201
00:07:59,959 --> 00:08:01,279
been sort of trained for.

202
00:08:01,279 --> 00:08:03,159
And then they're static out in the world.

203
00:08:03,159 --> 00:08:04,920
They need to learn on the fly.

204
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:08,280
There's quite a few, in my view, missing capabilities

205
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:10,720
that are quite critical to what I would

206
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:13,360
regard as an AGI system.

207
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:14,920
Google's a major investor in Anthropic.

208
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:17,600
Dario was here earlier today.

209
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:20,720
Do you agree or disagree with his prediction

210
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:24,439
that AI will wipe away 50% of entry-level white collar

211
00:08:24,439 --> 00:08:26,759
jobs in five years?

212
00:08:26,759 --> 00:08:30,400
I think that's also my timelines and my view on that

213
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,600
would be a lot longer.

214
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:33,960
I mean, I think we're starting to see maybe

215
00:08:33,960 --> 00:08:36,840
the beginnings of that this year in terms of maybe

216
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:41,080
entry-level jobs or internships, those types of things.

217
00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:44,320
But I think we would have to solve a lot more

218
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,920
of this consistency that AI doesn't have right now.

219
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:48,240
I call it jagged intelligences.

220
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:49,800
We're very good at certain things.

221
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:52,519
And it's very poor, the current systems, at other things.

222
00:08:52,519 --> 00:08:55,759
And if you want to offload or delegate an entire task

223
00:08:55,759 --> 00:08:59,759
to, say, an agent, rather than having what we have today,

224
00:08:59,759 --> 00:09:01,480
which are more like assistive programs,

225
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:03,639
you're going to need a lot more consistency across the board.

226
00:09:03,639 --> 00:09:06,559
It's no good for it to be good at 95% of that task.

227
00:09:06,559 --> 00:09:08,960
You need it to be good at the whole task

228
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:11,720
for you to be able to actually just sort of fire and forget

229
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:12,759
on it.

230
00:09:12,759 --> 00:09:15,039
So I think there's still quite a lot more

231
00:09:15,039 --> 00:09:17,399
to be done before we'll see that kind of disruption.

232
00:09:17,399 --> 00:09:19,600
But that kind of disruption will happen.

233
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:20,720
I think eventually, sure.

234
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:24,439
I mean, in the limit with AGI, if you have systems like that,

235
00:09:24,439 --> 00:09:26,399
I think that changes the whole economy, actually.

236
00:09:26,399 --> 00:09:28,399
It's way beyond the question of jobs.

237
00:09:28,399 --> 00:09:30,639
I think potentially, if we build it right,

238
00:09:30,639 --> 00:09:32,840
we're in a post-scarcity world where

239
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:34,800
we've solved some of the kind of fundamental root

240
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:39,920
nodes of the world, like energy sources, new, clean, renewable,

241
00:09:39,920 --> 00:09:42,200
basically free energy sources.

242
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:44,800
If we solve fusion, something like that,

243
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:46,840
with the help of AI, new materials,

244
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:49,960
I think we'll be five, 10 years past AGI,

245
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,440
we'll be in a radical, abundant world.

246
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:55,440
And so what does that mean to the economy

247
00:09:55,440 --> 00:09:58,200
and how society works, actually?

248
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:00,240
Before we get to a post-scarcity world, though,

249
00:10:00,240 --> 00:10:03,360
if we get there, there is so much anxiety

250
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:05,440
about what happens in between.

251
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:05,960
I'm a mom.

252
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:07,279
I know you have kids.

253
00:10:07,279 --> 00:10:11,200
Like, what scares you most for them?

254
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:12,960
What do you talk to them about?

255
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:14,840
What do you tell them that's coming?

256
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:17,600
I mean, I've just heard so many, oh my gosh, for college,

257
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:20,200
graduates are going to have such a hard time.

258
00:10:20,200 --> 00:10:21,440
Well, I don't know about that.

259
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:25,080
Look, I think it's going to be an age of disruption,

260
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:26,960
just like the Industrial Revolution was.

261
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:29,919
Maybe 10x of that, which is kind of unbelievable to think about,

262
00:10:30,879 --> 00:10:33,079
so I usually describe it as going to be 10 times bigger

263
00:10:33,079 --> 00:10:34,879
and 10 times faster than the Industrial Revolution.

264
00:10:34,879 --> 00:10:35,719
To your kids.

265
00:10:35,719 --> 00:10:36,240
100x.

266
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:38,439
So 100x of it.

267
00:10:38,439 --> 00:10:41,439
No, I say this to everyone, but I think that comes with it

268
00:10:41,439 --> 00:10:42,519
huge opportunities.

269
00:10:42,519 --> 00:10:45,679
And I also am just a big believer in human ingenuity.

270
00:10:45,679 --> 00:10:49,839
We're extremely adaptable because our minds are so general.

271
00:10:49,839 --> 00:10:51,439
The human mind is very general.

272
00:10:51,439 --> 00:10:54,159
We've adapted to look at the modern world around us.

273
00:10:54,159 --> 00:10:55,839
Our hunter-gatherer minds have managed

274
00:10:55,839 --> 00:10:57,799
to build modern civilization.

275
00:10:57,799 --> 00:10:59,519
So I think we'll adapt again.

276
00:10:59,519 --> 00:11:01,120
I think it's a little bit unprecedented

277
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:02,559
because of the speed of it.

278
00:11:02,559 --> 00:11:04,799
Usually it takes one generation or two generations

279
00:11:04,799 --> 00:11:06,799
for a transformation like this to happen,

280
00:11:06,799 --> 00:11:09,679
and the magnitude of the transformative power

281
00:11:09,679 --> 00:11:10,840
of this technology.

282
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:14,439
But I think the kids today, I'd be encouraging them

283
00:11:14,439 --> 00:11:18,600
to get incredibly proficient with these new tools

284
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:20,159
and native with them.

285
00:11:20,159 --> 00:11:24,039
And they're almost equivalent of giving them superpowers

286
00:11:24,039 --> 00:11:25,199
in the creative arts.

287
00:11:25,199 --> 00:11:29,159
You could probably do the job of what would have taken 10 people

288
00:11:29,399 --> 00:11:30,000
in one.

289
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
And I think that means if you're entrepreneurial,

290
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,799
if you're creative with game design, films, projects,

291
00:11:36,799 --> 00:11:38,319
you can probably get a lot more done

292
00:11:38,319 --> 00:11:40,839
and break into those industries a lot more easily

293
00:11:40,839 --> 00:11:45,439
than you could in the past as a new upcoming talent.

294
00:11:45,439 --> 00:11:48,120
Some folks have advocated for a pause

295
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:50,519
to give regulation time to catch up,

296
00:11:50,519 --> 00:11:55,159
to give society time to adjust to some of these changes.

297
00:11:55,159 --> 00:11:59,120
In a perfect world, if you knew that every other company would

298
00:11:59,159 --> 00:12:01,000
pause, if every country would pause,

299
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,200
would you advocate for that?

300
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:02,759
I think so.

301
00:12:02,759 --> 00:12:05,039
I mean, I've been on record saying what I'd like to see

302
00:12:05,039 --> 00:12:08,919
happen, it was always my dream of the kind of the roadmap

303
00:12:08,919 --> 00:12:11,720
at least I had when I started out DeepMind 15 years ago

304
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:14,679
and started working on AI 25 years ago now,

305
00:12:14,679 --> 00:12:17,639
was that as we got close to this moment,

306
00:12:17,639 --> 00:12:19,919
this threshold moment of AGI arriving,

307
00:12:21,399 --> 00:12:25,560
we would maybe collaborate in a scientific way.

308
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:28,560
I sometimes talk about setting up an international CERN

309
00:12:28,639 --> 00:12:32,639
equivalent for AI, where all the best minds in the world

310
00:12:32,639 --> 00:12:37,119
would collaborate together and do the final steps

311
00:12:37,119 --> 00:12:40,879
in a very rigorous scientific way, involving all of society,

312
00:12:40,879 --> 00:12:43,799
maybe philosophers and social scientists and economists,

313
00:12:43,799 --> 00:12:46,919
as well as technologists, to kind of figure out

314
00:12:46,919 --> 00:12:50,959
what we want from this technology and how to utilize it

315
00:12:50,959 --> 00:12:54,279
in a way that benefits all of humanity.

316
00:12:54,279 --> 00:12:56,399
And I think that's what's at stake.

317
00:12:56,439 --> 00:12:58,959
Unfortunately, it kind of needs international collaboration

318
00:12:58,959 --> 00:13:02,240
though, because even if one company or even one nation

319
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:06,519
or even the West decided to do that, it has no use

320
00:13:06,519 --> 00:13:09,079
unless the whole world agrees, at least on some kind

321
00:13:09,079 --> 00:13:10,399
of minimum standards.

322
00:13:10,399 --> 00:13:13,600
And international cooperation's a little bit tricky

323
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:16,279
at the moment, so that's gonna have to change

324
00:13:16,279 --> 00:13:21,199
if we want to have that kind of rigorous scientific approach

325
00:13:21,199 --> 00:13:22,960
to the final steps to AGI.

326
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:25,559
So if AGI comes in, let's say 2030,

327
00:13:25,599 --> 00:13:27,519
we don't have the regulations set up yet.

328
00:13:27,519 --> 00:13:30,759
Are we destined for something difficult?

329
00:13:30,759 --> 00:13:35,759
Well, then I think I'm still optimistic

330
00:13:35,879 --> 00:13:40,799
that enough of the leading players will kind of communicate

331
00:13:40,799 --> 00:13:43,039
together and hopefully collaborate, at least on things

332
00:13:43,039 --> 00:13:45,079
like safety and security protocols.

333
00:13:45,079 --> 00:13:45,959
There's a lot of that already.

334
00:13:45,959 --> 00:13:48,439
We work quite closely with Anthropic, for example,

335
00:13:48,439 --> 00:13:49,519
on those things.

336
00:13:49,519 --> 00:13:54,159
And that would be needed then, maybe kind of more

337
00:13:54,159 --> 00:13:56,919
peer-based cooperation if we can't get

338
00:13:56,919 --> 00:13:58,759
that international thing to work.

339
00:13:58,759 --> 00:14:00,600
But I would, that would be a lot more pressure.

340
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:03,480
You think it'd get Elon and Sam to cooperate with you?

341
00:14:03,480 --> 00:14:05,480
Potentially, I think I'm on pretty good terms

342
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:06,959
with pretty much all the other leaders

343
00:14:06,959 --> 00:14:07,879
of all the leading labs.

344
00:14:07,879 --> 00:14:09,959
I mean, I think if the stakes are high enough,

345
00:14:09,959 --> 00:14:14,240
and I think a lot of it is understanding what's at stake

346
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:15,600
and what the risks are.

347
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:18,439
And I think that will become clearer to everyone

348
00:14:18,439 --> 00:14:19,639
in the next two, three years.

349
00:14:19,639 --> 00:14:23,039
So let's talk about the technology and the next curve.

350
00:14:23,039 --> 00:14:26,599
Yan LeCun has said he doesn't think transformers and LLMs

351
00:14:26,599 --> 00:14:29,240
alone will get us to AGI.

352
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:30,959
Do you agree or disagree?

353
00:14:30,959 --> 00:14:34,399
And if they're dead ends, then what are we doing?

354
00:14:34,399 --> 00:14:35,319
Yes.

355
00:14:35,319 --> 00:14:39,480
No, I disagree that they're dead ends, but obviously,

356
00:14:39,480 --> 00:14:40,839
and I think that's clearly wrong.

357
00:14:40,839 --> 00:14:44,199
I mean, they're so amazingly useful already.

358
00:14:44,199 --> 00:14:47,079
But I think the way I say it is an empirical question.

359
00:14:47,079 --> 00:14:48,719
I think it's a scientific question

360
00:14:48,719 --> 00:14:52,039
whether they're going to be enough on their own.

361
00:14:52,039 --> 00:14:56,360
I think it's 50-50 that just scaling up existing methods

362
00:14:56,360 --> 00:14:57,719
with some tweaks will be enough.

363
00:14:57,719 --> 00:14:59,120
It might be.

364
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:00,399
And you have to do that.

365
00:15:00,399 --> 00:15:03,719
And I think that's useful work because at a minimum,

366
00:15:03,719 --> 00:15:06,719
the way I look at it is these LLMs will be a component,

367
00:15:06,719 --> 00:15:10,240
a massively important component of the final AGI system.

368
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:14,240
The only question in my mind is, is it the only component?

369
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:17,120
And I could imagine there are one or two breakthroughs,

370
00:15:17,120 --> 00:15:19,759
maybe a small handful, less than five,

371
00:15:19,759 --> 00:15:21,360
that are still needed from here.

372
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:22,200
Less than five.

373
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:22,720
Yes.

374
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:25,399
And so those might be things like world models.

375
00:15:25,399 --> 00:15:27,000
That's something that Yan talked about.

376
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:27,800
We're working on that.

377
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:29,759
In fact, we have the best world model currently,

378
00:15:29,759 --> 00:15:31,480
which is Genie, our Genie system.

379
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:34,080
And I work on that directly, and I think it's very important.

380
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:36,120
But also things like continual learning

381
00:15:36,120 --> 00:15:41,240
and having consistent systems that don't have these jagged

382
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:43,600
edges that they're good at and not good at.

383
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:45,440
A general system shouldn't have that.

384
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:48,440
So I think better reasoning, more long-term planning,

385
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,720
there's quite a few capabilities that are still missing.

386
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:55,400
And it's an open question whether a new architecture

387
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:57,840
or a new breakthrough is needed or more of the same.

388
00:15:57,840 --> 00:15:59,480
And we're just, from my point of view,

389
00:15:59,480 --> 00:16:00,720
from Google DeepMind's point of view,

390
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:03,240
we're pushing as hard as possible on both those things,

391
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:06,440
both inventing new things and scaling up existing things.

392
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:09,160
So slightly different, but related,

393
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:11,680
Ilya Suskova said the era of scaling and making bigger

394
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:15,720
models and make improvements is nearly over.

395
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:17,240
Is that something you agree with?

396
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:18,000
No, I don't agree.

397
00:16:18,159 --> 00:16:20,960
His exact quote was this, we're back to the age of research.

398
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:24,080
But I love Ilya, and we're very good friends.

399
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:25,720
And we agree on a lot of things.

400
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:27,919
But my view is we never left the age of research,

401
00:16:27,919 --> 00:16:29,600
at least from the point of view of DeepMind.

402
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:31,399
We've always invested.

403
00:16:31,399 --> 00:16:35,360
In my view, we've always had the deepest and broadest bench.

404
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:37,840
Actually, Google and DeepMind together,

405
00:16:37,840 --> 00:16:39,519
we, if you look over the last decade,

406
00:16:39,519 --> 00:16:42,039
we've invented about 90% of the breakthroughs

407
00:16:42,039 --> 00:16:44,840
that the modern industry relies on.

408
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:46,919
Of course, Transformers most famously,

409
00:16:46,959 --> 00:16:49,159
but also deep reinforcement learning, AlphaGo,

410
00:16:49,159 --> 00:16:51,279
these kinds of reinforcement learning techniques,

411
00:16:51,279 --> 00:16:52,679
we pioneered all of that.

412
00:16:52,679 --> 00:16:56,240
So if some new breakthroughs are required in the future,

413
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:59,919
I would back us to be the ones, just like in the past,

414
00:16:59,919 --> 00:17:03,519
to be the ones to make those breakthroughs in the future.

415
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:06,039
Last agree or disagree?

416
00:17:06,039 --> 00:17:09,680
Elon says we have entered the singularity.

417
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:11,639
No, I think that's very premature.

418
00:17:13,279 --> 00:17:16,480
I think the singularity is another word for

419
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:18,400
a full AGI arriving.

420
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:22,200
And I think I explained earlier why I think that we're still

421
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:23,720
nowhere near that.

422
00:17:23,720 --> 00:17:25,400
I think we will get there.

423
00:17:25,400 --> 00:17:27,279
And five years, even five years,

424
00:17:27,279 --> 00:17:29,000
it's not a long amount of time if you think about

425
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:29,960
what that is.

426
00:17:29,960 --> 00:17:32,279
But I think there's still a lot of work to be done

427
00:17:32,279 --> 00:17:34,720
before we have anything that looks like the singularity.

428
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:36,079
So talk to us a little bit about the culture

429
00:17:36,079 --> 00:17:41,079
inside Google right now to win this race, but do it right.

430
00:17:42,599 --> 00:17:45,720
The leadership, how involved are Larry and Sergey right now?

431
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:48,720
How often do you talk to them and what are their priorities?

432
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:49,920
Yeah, they're very involved.

433
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:52,640
And Larry Moore on the strategic side,

434
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:54,319
see him at board meetings and other times

435
00:17:54,319 --> 00:17:55,839
when I visit the Valley.

436
00:17:55,839 --> 00:17:58,240
Sergey's more hands-on on the,

437
00:17:58,240 --> 00:18:00,079
he's involved in the coding of,

438
00:18:00,079 --> 00:18:01,600
on the Gemini team specifically,

439
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:05,440
more in the algorithmic details.

440
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:07,880
And it's great having them both energized around

441
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:10,480
where we are and who wouldn't be at this moment,

442
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:12,759
absolutely incredible moment for computer science.

443
00:18:12,759 --> 00:18:14,400
So just from a scientific point of view,

444
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:15,560
which both of them are,

445
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:18,920
it's an incredibly exciting moment in history,

446
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:20,240
human history really.

447
00:18:20,240 --> 00:18:23,880
And so of course, everyone wants to be hands-on

448
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:24,920
and heavily involved in that.

449
00:18:24,920 --> 00:18:26,400
So that's great.

450
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:30,680
And for us, just as an entity,

451
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:32,640
I'm trying to combine the best of many worlds.

452
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:35,360
So, startup energy of shipping things fast

453
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:37,680
and taking risks and doing things like that,

454
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:39,920
which I think you're seeing the benefits of,

455
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:43,720
the big company resources is amazing, useful,

456
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:47,400
but also protecting the space for long-term research still

457
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:48,920
and exploratory research,

458
00:18:48,920 --> 00:18:52,039
not just researching what we'll deliver in,

459
00:18:52,039 --> 00:18:53,160
three months in a product.

460
00:18:53,160 --> 00:18:54,720
I think that would be a mistake too.

461
00:18:54,720 --> 00:18:56,160
So I'm trying to balance all of those

462
00:18:56,160 --> 00:18:57,759
different factors together.

463
00:18:57,759 --> 00:19:01,079
And I think in the last year, things have been going well.

464
00:19:01,079 --> 00:19:02,360
I think we can still do better.

465
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:04,600
And I think we still will do better this year,

466
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,519
but I'm very happy with our trajectory.

467
00:19:06,519 --> 00:19:09,400
I think it's the steepest trajectory of improvement

468
00:19:09,400 --> 00:19:11,880
and progress of anyone in the industry.

469
00:19:11,880 --> 00:19:13,760
You are a Nobel laureate,

470
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:15,680
and I know how obsessed you are with

471
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:18,560
AI powering scientific research.

472
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:22,120
If AI itself, let's say, makes a Nobel-worthy discovery,

473
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:23,160
who should get the prize?

474
00:19:23,160 --> 00:19:24,560
The AI or the human?

475
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:26,400
I think still the human, I would say,

476
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:28,280
because I feel like, I mean,

477
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:31,520
it depends what you mean by completely on its own, right?

478
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:34,000
So for now, these are still tools,

479
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,640
and I view them as like very,

480
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:39,360
maybe the ultimate scientific tool,

481
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:41,640
but sort of better versions of telescopes

482
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:43,160
and microscopes.

483
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:44,360
We've always built tools so that we can investigate

484
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:46,000
the natural world better.

485
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,200
We're tool-making animals, basically.

486
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:51,200
That's what distinguishes humans from the other animals.

487
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:52,720
And that's our superpower.

488
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:55,360
And I include computers in that, of course,

489
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:57,680
and AI being the ultimate expression of that.

490
00:19:57,680 --> 00:19:59,240
So in some ways, I think of AI,

491
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:00,360
and I've always thought of it,

492
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:02,880
as the ultimate tool to do science.

493
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:05,800
And I think for the foreseeable future,

494
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:09,000
that's gonna be a collaboration with top scientists,

495
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:10,480
putting in the creative ideas,

496
00:20:10,480 --> 00:20:13,079
and maybe the hypotheses with these amazing tools

497
00:20:13,079 --> 00:20:16,480
that enhance data processing and pattern matching

498
00:20:16,480 --> 00:20:19,120
and the exploration part of science.

499
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:22,960
You obviously could have sold DeepMind to anyone.

500
00:20:24,759 --> 00:20:26,400
And I think all of these companies

501
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:29,480
are asking a lot of us to trust you,

502
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:33,480
especially if regulation doesn't keep up with technology,

503
00:20:33,480 --> 00:20:35,599
which history shows it probably won't.

504
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,720
Why should we trust you,

505
00:20:38,720 --> 00:20:40,400
and why do you think Google,

506
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:42,240
which I would implicitly think you believe,

507
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:47,240
is the place that we should believe in the most

508
00:20:47,360 --> 00:20:49,880
when it comes to something that feels so risky?

509
00:20:49,880 --> 00:20:52,799
Yes, I think you need to judge these companies

510
00:20:52,799 --> 00:20:57,000
by their actions and also look into the motivations,

511
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:00,799
I would say, of the leaders involved in those endeavors.

512
00:21:00,799 --> 00:21:02,799
And for me and for us, and that's one reason

513
00:21:02,799 --> 00:21:05,079
I picked Google as the right home for DeepMind,

514
00:21:05,079 --> 00:21:06,480
is several reasons.

515
00:21:06,519 --> 00:21:09,079
The main one being that the founders of Google

516
00:21:09,079 --> 00:21:10,839
and the way Google was set up by them

517
00:21:10,839 --> 00:21:12,799
was as a scientific company.

518
00:21:12,799 --> 00:21:15,759
Many people forget Google itself was a PhD project, right?

519
00:21:15,759 --> 00:21:17,839
It was Larry and Sergey's PhD project.

520
00:21:17,839 --> 00:21:21,039
So I felt an immediate affinity with them,

521
00:21:21,039 --> 00:21:23,079
and Larry led the acquisition,

522
00:21:23,079 --> 00:21:25,120
but also the board, who they collected on the board.

523
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:26,559
You have John Hennessey, who's the chair,

524
00:21:26,559 --> 00:21:28,559
who's a Turing Award winner himself,

525
00:21:28,559 --> 00:21:30,880
and Francis Arnold, another Nobel Prize winner.

526
00:21:30,880 --> 00:21:33,559
These are unusual people to have on a corporate board.

527
00:21:33,559 --> 00:21:38,000
So the whole environment is very scientific,

528
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,079
and science and research led,

529
00:21:40,079 --> 00:21:41,960
and engineering led as a culture,

530
00:21:41,960 --> 00:21:43,799
and it's deeply ingrained in the culture.

531
00:21:43,799 --> 00:21:46,480
And that means doing science at the highest level

532
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:48,919
means being really rigorous, being thoughtful,

533
00:21:48,919 --> 00:21:51,399
and applying the scientific method everywhere you can.

534
00:21:51,399 --> 00:21:52,919
I think that's not just to the technology,

535
00:21:52,919 --> 00:21:55,679
but it's also to the way you operate as an organization.

536
00:21:55,679 --> 00:21:59,639
So I feel like we're very,

537
00:21:59,639 --> 00:22:02,240
we try to be very thoughtful and responsible

538
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:04,200
and have as much foresight as possible

539
00:22:04,200 --> 00:22:06,319
over the technologies we put out in the world.

540
00:22:06,319 --> 00:22:07,839
Doesn't mean we'll get everything right,

541
00:22:07,839 --> 00:22:10,799
because it's so complex and so new and nascent

542
00:22:10,799 --> 00:22:12,599
and so transformative, this technology,

543
00:22:12,599 --> 00:22:15,120
but we hope to coast correct as quickly as possible

544
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:17,319
if something does go wrong.

545
00:22:17,319 --> 00:22:20,400
And then the final thing I would say is just the,

546
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:22,720
I was just attracted by the types of things

547
00:22:22,720 --> 00:22:24,359
Google tries to do in the world.

548
00:22:24,359 --> 00:22:26,120
Organizing the world's information,

549
00:22:26,120 --> 00:22:28,480
I think is a very noble goal,

550
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:31,079
which is obviously Google's mission statement.

551
00:22:31,119 --> 00:22:33,159
And I think it fitted very well with that,

552
00:22:33,159 --> 00:22:35,879
with DeepMind's mission statement of solving intelligence

553
00:22:35,879 --> 00:22:37,799
and using it to solve everything else.

554
00:22:37,799 --> 00:22:41,839
And that was our, and I think those two mission statements

555
00:22:41,839 --> 00:22:45,240
are natural fit, AI and organizing the world's information

556
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:46,519
naturally go together.

557
00:22:46,519 --> 00:22:48,960
And I think those are the types of products,

558
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:51,000
the types of products that Google's well known for,

559
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:55,119
from maps to Gmail to obviously search.

560
00:22:55,119 --> 00:22:57,639
I think they're genuinely useful products in the world.

561
00:22:57,639 --> 00:22:59,599
And I think AI is an easy fit

562
00:22:59,599 --> 00:23:02,480
how that would work with those products to enhance them

563
00:23:02,480 --> 00:23:04,399
for everybody to use in their everyday lives.

564
00:23:04,399 --> 00:23:06,319
And I think that's a great thing for the world.

565
00:23:06,319 --> 00:23:09,319
So, I'm happy to be contributing to that.

566
00:23:09,319 --> 00:23:10,919
Okay, so post-scarcity world,

567
00:23:10,919 --> 00:23:12,719
where there are people no longer have jobs,

568
00:23:12,719 --> 00:23:16,480
what do you personally plan to do with your time

569
00:23:16,480 --> 00:23:19,000
once you have achieved all your technical goals,

570
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,199
the research is just automating itself?

571
00:23:21,199 --> 00:23:24,439
Right, well, I would love to use it for,

572
00:23:24,439 --> 00:23:27,119
what I will do post the singularity is to use it

573
00:23:27,119 --> 00:23:29,919
for exploring the limits of physics.

574
00:23:29,919 --> 00:23:32,639
I think that was my favorite subject at school

575
00:23:32,639 --> 00:23:34,639
was the big questions.

576
00:23:34,639 --> 00:23:35,959
What is the fabric of reality?

577
00:23:35,959 --> 00:23:37,399
What's the nature of reality?

578
00:23:37,399 --> 00:23:39,959
What about the nature of consciousness?

579
00:23:39,959 --> 00:23:42,879
The answer to the Fermi paradox, all of these things,

580
00:23:42,879 --> 00:23:44,159
what is time, what is gravity?

581
00:23:44,159 --> 00:23:47,639
I'm amazed more people, we just go around our daily lives,

582
00:23:47,639 --> 00:23:49,919
not really thinking about these massive questions

583
00:23:49,919 --> 00:23:53,239
that for me are always kind of almost screaming at me

584
00:23:53,239 --> 00:23:55,439
for like, what is the answer to these things,

585
00:23:55,439 --> 00:23:56,759
these deep mysteries?

586
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:01,160
I would like to use AI and to explore all of those things,

587
00:24:01,160 --> 00:24:03,240
maybe traveling to the stars as well,

588
00:24:03,240 --> 00:24:06,519
with the help of new energy sources and materials

589
00:24:06,519 --> 00:24:08,200
and other things that's unlocked by AI.

590
00:24:08,200 --> 00:24:10,599
Will we all have meaning and purpose if we don't have work?

591
00:24:10,599 --> 00:24:12,559
Well, I think that's the, to be honest with you,

592
00:24:12,559 --> 00:24:14,799
that's the thing I worry more about than the economics.

593
00:24:14,799 --> 00:24:17,920
I think the economics is almost a political question of like,

594
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:20,319
when we get all of these extra benefits and productivity,

595
00:24:20,319 --> 00:24:22,440
can we make sure that it's shared

596
00:24:23,480 --> 00:24:24,799
for the benefit of everyone?

597
00:24:24,839 --> 00:24:27,159
And I think obviously that's what I believe in.

598
00:24:27,159 --> 00:24:29,000
But then the bigger question than that is,

599
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,079
what about purpose and meaning that a lot of us get

600
00:24:31,079 --> 00:24:33,839
from our jobs and scientific endeavors?

601
00:24:33,839 --> 00:24:36,440
How will we find that in the new world?

602
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:39,319
And I think, we all need some new great philosophers

603
00:24:39,319 --> 00:24:43,919
in my opinion, to help with that and thinking that through.

604
00:24:43,919 --> 00:24:46,399
Maybe we'll be getting much more sophisticated

605
00:24:46,399 --> 00:24:49,799
with our art and exploration that we do

606
00:24:49,799 --> 00:24:51,639
in things like extreme sports.

607
00:24:51,639 --> 00:24:53,079
There's many things we do today

608
00:24:53,079 --> 00:24:55,039
that aren't just for economic gain.

609
00:24:55,039 --> 00:24:57,079
And perhaps we'll have very esoteric versions

610
00:24:57,079 --> 00:24:58,319
of those things in the future.

611
00:24:58,319 --> 00:24:59,879
All right, so everyone in the room is wondering

612
00:24:59,879 --> 00:25:00,720
what they should be doing.

613
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:02,359
Like, what should I do about AI?

614
00:25:02,359 --> 00:25:03,720
What do I do?

615
00:25:03,720 --> 00:25:06,359
Sitting here in Davos, in 10 years,

616
00:25:07,279 --> 00:25:09,240
what is the biggest mistake do you think people

617
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:12,119
in this room will have made about AI?

618
00:25:12,119 --> 00:25:14,119
Well, look, I think there's two things I would say.

619
00:25:14,119 --> 00:25:17,279
One is for the younger generation and our kids and so on,

620
00:25:17,279 --> 00:25:19,119
is the only thing we're certain of is,

621
00:25:19,119 --> 00:25:20,879
there's gonna be a huge amount of change.

622
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:23,200
So I think in terms of learning skills,

623
00:25:23,200 --> 00:25:26,240
get ready to kind of learning to learn

624
00:25:26,240 --> 00:25:27,400
is the most important thing.

625
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:29,840
How can quickly can you adapt to new situations

626
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:32,440
absorb new information using the tools that we have.

627
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:35,280
For the CEOs and business folks in the room,

628
00:25:35,280 --> 00:25:38,080
I think the most important thing to do now is,

629
00:25:38,080 --> 00:25:39,960
there are many providers of leading models

630
00:25:39,960 --> 00:25:42,000
and leading service providers,

631
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,440
and there'll be more for these AI models.

632
00:25:44,440 --> 00:25:48,840
Pick the partners that you feel are approaching it

633
00:25:48,840 --> 00:25:49,840
in the right way.

634
00:25:49,839 --> 00:25:53,159
And so, kind of partner with those that are making

635
00:25:53,159 --> 00:25:55,319
the changes and approaching this technology

636
00:25:55,319 --> 00:25:57,399
in the way that you would like to see in the world.

637
00:25:57,399 --> 00:26:00,439
And I think together we can kind of build that future

638
00:26:00,439 --> 00:26:03,240
with AI coming down the line that we all want.

